# TODO - Autonomous AI quiz publication

## Objective

Finish admitted AI image delivery, autonomous publication, correction,
progress, cancellation, and recovery through the canonical operations.

## Acceptance

**IT WORKS** when the teacher can ask an AI client for a quiz and the
registered product tools produce, publish, and verify it on Blooket without
requiring a routine editorial approval step. The flow must support admitted
question options and stable-ID media, show understandable progress/errors, and
correct supported mismatches without duplicate publication.

Actual attachment intake must work through an explicit, bounded path when media
is requested; do not assume ChatGPT attachments automatically arrive at MCP.
A genuine login/challenge/permission stop still requires the human. Show one
complete teacher-facing journey and focused checks; defer extra UX polish,
rare edge cases, and platform-only assurance to ongoing hardening.

## Implementation notes

Apply the two-field intake and normalization requirements from tasks 05-07 to
AI-assisted review. The AI supplies detected language, topics, and structured
normalized English metadata through admitted enrichment operations, preserving
the teacher's original name and description. Canonical asset paths remain
service-owned. Import, English source text, or an unsaved generated response is
not completed normalization.

Expose validated quiz list/read/create/edit, question type/seconds/media,
progress, and recovery through registered CLI commands and MCP tools. Keep local
JSON as automatic private drafts/execution state rather than requiring manual
file management or treating it as authority over fresh Blooket state.

Finish autonomous quiz publication, progress, cancellation, correction, and
recovery in the browser UI. Manual inspection remains available but is not a
pre-publication gate. Translate remaining states and actionable errors into
English and Spanish, preserving quiz and original-media language independently.

## Evidence

On 2026-10-21, an actual UI error-path regression confirmed that known
single-question correction errors were classified as unknown generic stops.
The Studio now explains a required one-row change and unsupported edit types
in both supported UI languages, while uncertain remote baselines remain a
conflict stop. This improves repair guidance without invoking Blooket.

A 2026-10-21 red-to-green UI regression found that a contradictory initial
`not-started` status could claim a remote set ID or a completed operation.
The publication orchestration now refuses both before issuing its first
`blooket.publication.step`, preserving the canonical journal as authority.
This is a portable fail-closed guarantee, not unattended live acceptance.

On 2026-10-21, registered MCP image import, enrichment and preparation ran
through actual CLI subprocesses against the development library. The new
`library_image_prepare` returned a 24,434-byte current-revision rendition;
`library_get` confirmed completed normalization and preserved original Spanish
name/description. No local editor or caller-selected path was needed.

Separate registered MCP verification confirmed the recovered two-question
panel journey at 03:59 UTC. That journey needed controller assistance at two
provider 429 stops; it does not demonstrate uninterrupted autonomous creation.
The earlier autonomous existing-question correction has positive verification
at 03:32 UTC. Browserless agents must retain these evidence distinctions.

On 2026-10-20, a new bounded `library_image_import` MCP tool now accepts
explicit client-supplied canonical base64 (up to 900,000 encoded characters)
and preserves original name/description under a service-owned media ID. An
identical replay reuses its prior asset, and unknown path fields are refused.
Its actual MCP-to-CLI-to-library integration passed with a generated image;
this is not proof that arbitrary ChatGPT attachments are automatically
forwarded to MCP. Local Studio intake remains the path for larger images.

The independent real existing-question correction is still blocked by the
uncertain 429-limited provider attempt, so this record stays open.

